.TH "ACTIVITY" "1" "November 24, 2026" "ka9q-radio" "ka9q-radio Manual"
.SH NAME
activity \- log transmissions anywhere in radiod's coverage, configured or not
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B activity
.RI "[\-c config ...] [\-l log.csv] [\-s summary.csv] [\-u coverage.csv] [\-t dB] [\-m seconds] [\-S kHz] [\-D dB] [\-v] radiod-status-group"
.SH DESCRIPTION
activity watches everything radiod's front end covers (a Fobos at 50 MHz:
about 422 to 469 MHz) through a spectrum channel of its own, 6.25 kHz bins
nominally four times a second, and logs each transmission it finds,
whatever its tone or mode. Each look is the average of as many FFTs as
radiod holds, nominally the last 80 ms: the band is sampled, not watched
continuously. A transmission of half a second or more (the shortest logged)
is sampled as long as radiod answers promptly (a late answer delays the
next look, up to a second); a shorter one may fall between looks. Whether a
sampled transmission is found depends on its level: a DMR or NXDN burst, on
for about half of a look, reads about 3 dB below its carrier, so a weak one
may stay under the threshold.
.P
Each bin has a noise floor of its own, falling at once to a quieter
reading and rising over half an hour, so a strong station's skirt isn't
taken for activity, and a carrier that never stops is logged once and then
taken into the floor. Bins more than 10 dB above their floors, side by side,
make a signal. A signal heard alongside one at least 20 dB stronger within
150 kHz (\-D, \-S) is taken for that one's skirt or splatter (a
transmitter in the house, a repeater down the road), not a transmission of
its own. A signal at the same frequency continues a transmission, and
2 seconds without it ends it; anything under half a second is noise.
.P
Times in the log, summary and coverage are UTC, marked Z (2026-11-24
13:29:20Z), so a span across a daylight saving change keeps its length.
The log (activity.csv) has a line per transmission: its start and end,
frequency (the signals' power-weighted centre) and the nearest 6.25 kHz
channel, width, length, how far above the floor and its level in dBFS,
the configured name if it is on a known frequency (within 5 kHz), and a
guess at the kind of signal from its width alone. The summary
(activity-summary.csv), rewritten every minute and at the end, gives each
channel's transmissions, time on the air, first and last heard and name;
at a restart it is rebuilt from the log, so it covers the whole log.
.P
The coverage file (activity-coverage.csv, by default) records when activity was
watching, and what: a row per stretch of continuous monitoring, its start,
end and the span watched (lo_mhz, hi_mhz). A stretch ends after 5 seconds
without a spectrum (radiod gone, say), when the span changes (the front
end retuned), after ten minutes (rows are written as they close, so a
crash loses at most that) and when activity stops. With it,
frequency-survey.py says how long a quiet frequency was actually watched.
.P
Known frequencies are every frequency in the configuration files given
(freq lists, and trunked systems' nxdn-channels, ltr-repeaters and control
channels), named by their sections, and radiod's own channels, including
the voice channels its followers are on. A transmission on none of them is
logged with its name empty.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B "\-c config"
A radiod configuration file, or a directory of them (*.conf), for the
known frequencies and their names. May be given more than once.
.TP
.B "\-l file"
The log. Default activity.csv.
.TP
.B "\-s file"
The summary. Default activity-summary.csv. It must not be the log.
.TP
.B "\-u file"
The coverage file. Default: the log's name with -coverage
(activity-coverage.csv; uhf.csv: uhf-coverage.csv), where
frequency-survey.py looks for it. The log, summary and coverage must be
three different files (hard links included).
.TP
.B "\-t dB"
How far above its floor a bin must be. Default 10.
.TP
.B "\-m seconds"
The shortest transmission logged. Default 0.5.
.TP
.B "\-S kHz"
How near a stronger signal a weaker one is taken for its skirt. Default
150; 0 turns the rule off. Only signals kept can shadow others (the
strongest first), so the distance is always from a real signal; and a
transmission already followed goes on through a shadow, not split by it.
.TP
.B "\-D dB"
How much stronger (in dBFS) that one must be. Default 20.
.TP
.B "\-v"
Print each transmission as it ends; twice, each spectrum's strongest bin.
.SH EXAMPLE
.nf
activity \-v \-c ~/Frequencies/uhf.d fobos-uhf.local
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
radiod(8), waterfall(1), powers(1)
